Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Great progress this week, but your legs need urgent attention to keep your physique balanced.",
  "summary": "Solid week of training overall! Chest and back both showed strong strength gains, and your upper body volume was well-distributed across the major pushing and pulling muscles. Your total tonnage of 8,732 kg is a healthy output for your body weight, and the progressive overload trend is encouraging. The one significant concern is legs, which received only 1 set and just 4.6% of your total volume — far below what's needed for balanced muscle development. Addressing this imbalance will be the key priority heading into next week.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "Chest improved from 104 to 108 and back from 104 to 107 — both solid jumps that indicate meaningful progressive overload on your primary upper-body lifts. Shoulders ticked up slightly from 102 to 103 and triceps from 103 to 104, showing steady if modest gains. Biceps held flat at 101, which is not alarming for one period but worth monitoring if it continues. Legs remained flat at 100, though with only 1 set logged this is more a volume problem than a strength stall — there is simply not enough stimulus to drive adaptation."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
