      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "severe",
      "observation": "Only 1 set logged this period, representing just 4.6% of total volume. For muscle-building goals, legs typically require 10–20+ sets per week. This level of neglect risks both aesthetic imbalance and long-term hormonal and metabolic drawbacks from undertrained large muscle groups."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "3 sets and a flat strength index at 101 suggest biceps are receiving minimal dedicated stimulus. While some bicep work occurs through back training, adding one or two more direct sets would help drive hypertrophy in line with your goal."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Prioritize legs immediately — aim for at least 6 to 10 sets of leg work next period, incorporating compound movements like squats, leg press, or Romanian deadlifts to stimulate the large lower-body muscle groups you are currently undertrained in.",
    "Add 1 to 2 more direct bicep sets next period, such as an extra curl variation at the end of a back session, to get the strength index moving upward from its current stall at 101.",
    "Maintain the current chest and back volume and loading — both muscle groups are showing the strongest strength index gains this period, so keep the progression going without major changes.",
    "Monitor your recovery closely as you add leg volume; the sudden increase in lower-body stimulus may cause more fatigue than usual, so ensure you are getting adequate sleep and nutrition to support muscle growth across all groups."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strong upper-body week with chest and back leading strength gains, but legs were severely undertrained at just 1 set — the top priority next period is dramatically increasing leg volume to restore balance and support the long-term muscle-building goal."
}
